## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Draw a single picture that represents a simple story or event. Communication: Use gestures to show how to play a very simple game.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can clarify or confirm understanding of weather by pointing to pictures or using simple words. Can draw simple pictures representing the weather and label them with help. Can use gestures or body language to describe weather (e.g., pretending to shiver when it's cold).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a familiar "Weather Song" with Total Physical Response (TPR) actions for "sunny," "rainy," "cloudy," and "windy." Students sing and perform gestures. Teacher uses weather flashcards to review by asking, "What is the weather today?" and students point to the correct card. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing weather using simple sentences and gestures: "It is sunny!" (teacher makes a sun gesture), "It is raining!" (teacher makes rain falling gesture). The teacher then shows a simple, pre-made weather wheel and models how to point to a weather picture and say its name, mediating the visual to the verbal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a "sunny" flashcard: "Is it rainy?" (No!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher makes a shivering gesture: "Am I hot or cold?" (Cold!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a larger, classroom-sized "Weather Wheel" with clear pictures for "sunny," "rainy," "cloudy," and "windy." The teacher demonstrates how to use the pointer on the wheel to indicate the daily weather, explaining it as a tool to "show and tell" the weather. This acts as the "simple story or event" students will later represent.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful classroom "Weather Wheel" (visual aid, realia) with a movable arrow. Teacher's simple, repetitive spoken instructions. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Talking about weather: Describe simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny."). Sociolinguistic: Engaging in simple weather-related conversations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks, "Show me 'sunny'!" or "Show me 'rainy'!" Students take turns coming to the board and pointing the arrow on the classroom Weather Wheel to the correct weather picture. The teacher emphasizes the initial sound of /w/ in "weather" and /s/ in "sunny."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Present continuous (e.g., "It is raining."), Subject + verb + adjective (e.g., "It is sunny."). Vocabulary: weather, sun, rain, clouds, wind, sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, warm, cold. Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "raining," /w/ in "weather."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Weather Picture Match:</b> The teacher provides students with pre-printed circles of different weather pictures (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and corresponding word labels (or simple pictures that can be drawn). Teacher says a weather word (e.g., "sunny"). Students find the correct picture, hold it up, and say, "It is sunny!" (Mediating word to picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Draw and Show:</b> Students receive blank paper circles. The teacher says, "Draw 'windy' weather." Students draw simple pictures representing windy weather. After drawing, they hold up their drawing and, with teacher help, say, "It is windy!" The teacher monitors and helps with labeling. (Mediating verbal instruction to drawing and then to verbal description).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Gesture and Guess:</b> In pairs, one student secretly chooses a weather type (e.g., cold). They use gestures or body language (e.g., pretending to shiver for cold) to describe the weather. The partner points to a corresponding weather picture on their desk (or the picture they drew in activity 2) and says, "It is cold!" (Mediating gesture to picture and simple words).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Weather Wheel Explanation."</b> Students receive pre-cut components for their individual "Weather Wheel" (a base circle, weather pictures: sunny, rainy, windy, and an arrow). They assemble their wheels, gluing the weather pictures onto the base and attaching the arrow. Once assembled, in small groups or to the teacher, each student takes turns rotating their weather wheel's arrow to a specific weather condition, pointing to it, and clearly stating, "It is [weather adjective]." They are encouraged to add a gesture (e.g., making a sun shape with hands for "sunny," fluttering fingers for "rainy," blowing for "windy"). This is a direct mediation of their creation (the wheel) to a verbal explanation and gestural description.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students imagine they are the weather reporter for the day, explaining "Nice Weather Today!" or other weather conditions using their personal weather wheel. They practice asking "What is the weather today?" and answering using their wheel.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> Emphasis is on communicating the weather message clearly using their wheel, simple words, and gestures, rather than perfect grammar. Peers offer simple feedback by pointing or nodding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student performance during the "Weather Wheel Showcase."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Weather Wheel Showcase."</b> Each student will present their completed "Weather Wheel" to the teacher or a small group. They must:
                <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to at least two different weather conditions on their wheel (e.g., "sunny," "rainy").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe each chosen weather condition using a simple sentence (e.g., "It is sunny.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use a gesture or body language to further describe the weather (e.g., shining for sun, shivering for cold).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If prompted, clarify what a weather condition means by pointing again or using a different gesture.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can clarify or confirm understanding of weather by pointing to pictures or using simple words on the wheel.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use gestures or body language to describe weather (e.g., pretending to shiver when it's cold).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draws (or correctly places pre-drawn) simple pictures representing the weather and labels them (with help).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "rainy").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy or sad face next to these statements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can show 'sunny' on my weather wheel.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can use my hands to show 'rainy'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about showing the weather with your wheel today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What weather can you describe with your hands (gestures)?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully created and presented their weather wheels, mediating between the visual representation and verbal/gestural descriptions. The hands-on creation and presentation were highly engaging for 1st graders. Some students needed more prompting for gestures, indicating a need for more TPR practice. The mediation objective of drawing/labeling and clarifying understanding by pointing was achieved. The connection to the "It's Sunny!" theme was well-integrated. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This concludes the weather unit. The "Weather Wheel" project will be displayed in the classroom and can be used as a daily warm-up or routine activity to continuously reinforce weather vocabulary and mediation skills in future lessons or units, connecting English to daily life.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4-6 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>